body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

I noticed that the [enqueue a custom element callback reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-a-custom-element-callback-reaction) algorithm has a problem.

**The gist:** For the same element, step 6 can be performed multiple times, which will lead to a growing [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) or [backup element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#backup-element-queue), which will be filled with the same element, which is probably not what the spec wanted.
> 6. [Enqueue an element on the appropriate element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-an-element-on-the-appropriate-element-queue) given element.

**When is this possible?**

This is possible when the HTML parser creates a custom element with attributes that are observed (look at the [create an element for a token](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#create-an-element-for-the-token) algorithm in the step 10).

For example:
```html
<script>
customElements.define("a-element", class extends HTMLElement {
    static get observedAttributes() {
        return ["a","b","c"];
    }
    attributeChangedCallback(...args){
        console.log(this, args[0]);
    }
});
</script>
<a-element a b c></a-element>
```
That is, the situation after adding attributes to the `a-element` element will be as follows:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/52ccf68d-bcb0-4689-a56d-43dfd81f191a)

This happens because the element is added to two queues:
> 5. Add a new [callback reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#callback-reaction) to element's [custom element reaction queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reaction-queue), with callback function callback and arguments args.
> 6. [Enqueue an element on the appropriate element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-an-element-on-the-appropriate-element-queue) given element.

As a result, when the [invoke custom element reactions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#invoke-custom-element-reactions) algorithm is called, the reactions for the first element will be executed first (they are removed from the queue before execution), and there will simply be no reactions left for the rest elements, since all elements in the [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) are the same element.

This is what it looks like after the reactions are executed for the first element in the [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) (it has already been popped of the queue):

<img height=210px src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/265c673c-4c6d-46c9-9ad8-e9995058c69a"/>

---

We can also consider the case where [customElements.define](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#dom-customelementregistry-define) is called for `a-element`, which calls [upgrade reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#upgrade-reaction).

Example:

```html
<a-element a b c></a-element>
<script>
customElements.define("a-element", class extends HTMLElement {
    static get observedAttributes() {
        return ["a","b","c"];
    }
    attributeChangedCallback(...args){
        console.log(this, args[0]);
    }
    connectedCallback(){
        console.log(this, "connectedCallback")
    }
});
</script>
```

When the time comes to execute the [invoke custom element reactions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#invoke-custom-element-reactions) algorithm, there will be only one reaction and it is [upgrade reaction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#upgrade-reaction).

It looks like this:

<img height=250px src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/641a1a5e-5769-4ec5-b35a-fec9645bed1a"/>

By running this reaction we will run the [upgrade an element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#concept-upgrade-an-element) algorithm (in this case the [custom element reactions stack](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reactions-stack) will be empty), which will set several reactions related to attributes and one related to connecting the element to the DOM.

It is also worth noting that the queue was replenished when there was an empty [custom element reactions stack](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reactions-stack), which means that when we executed the [enqueue an element on the appropriate element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#enqueue-an-element-on-the-appropriate-element-queue) algorithm, we replenished the [backup element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#backup-element-queue) for each reaction, and also scheduled one microtask (due to the set flag [processing the backup element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#processing-the-backup-element-queue)).

It looks like this:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/474ece70-6358-4ab0-8b54-a21634e695cb)

But we perform these reactions without leaving the [invoke custom element reactions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#invoke-custom-element-reactions) algorithm, due to the replenishment of the [custom element reaction queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#custom-element-reaction-queue).

When the moment comes to execute our microtask to perform reactions for elements, it will do nothing, it will essentially be an empty call. Since all reactions for the element were called in the [invoke custom element reactions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#invoke-custom-element-reactions) algorithm for the [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) earlier.

What we have at the time of microtask execution:

<img height=160px src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a5888778-676b-4865-9b20-86e941d7b5cc"/>

---

As a result, we have a logic where [element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-queue) or [backup element queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#backup-element-queue) can add the same element, which pollutes the queue and creates extra work.

@domenic @annevk what do you think?
